6	 * the world — and on the stored example it is the oil price feeding back on an
7	 * output decision that then pushes the oil price the other way.
8	 *
9	 * Left to the ordinary router, that arrow takes the shortest orthogonal path
10	 * back across the map and passes straight through whatever tile is in the way.
11	 * On the stored example it ran behind the dead-end tile, which made that tile
12	 * look as though it had a wire leaving it towards the two tradeable endings —
13	 * a picture that says something untrue. So this one wire is routed over the
14	 * top of the map instead, where there is nothing to cross.
15	 *
16	 * **This is a placeholder and it is meant to be replaced.** The wires are about
17	 * to carry five things at once — what kind of push this is, how hard, whether
18	 * it has to keep holding, whether it loops back, and where it came from — and a
19	 * feedback arrow's proper drawing is a loop with the delay written on it. The
20	 * dashed stroke below says one thing only, *this one runs backwards*, and the
21	 * work that gives the stroke its real job takes it away again.
22	 */
23	
24	import { BaseEdge, type Edge, type EdgeProps } from "@xyflow/react";
25	
26	/** What a feedback wire needs beyond its two ends. */
27	export interface FeedbackWireData extends Record<string, unknown> {
28	  /**
29	   * The line the wire travels along, above every tile on the map.
30	   *
31	   * Worked out from the layout — the top of the highest tile, less a gap — and
32	   * handed in, because an edge cannot see where the other tiles are.
33	   */
